# Extension: Machine Learning Models

## Regression Tree Analysis

A regression tree model was fitted to capture potential nonlinearities and interaction effects among the predictors.

**Why Regression Trees?**  
Regression trees can automatically detect and model nonlinear interactions without requiring explicit specification. They are highly interpretable, showing clear decision rules.

**Assumptions:**  
- No assumptions of linearity or normality
- Data is partitioned recursively based on feature thresholds

**Limitations:**  
Regression trees are prone to overfitting and can be sensitive to small changes in the data. Pruning or ensemble methods are needed to stabilize performance.



```{r}
library(rpart)
library(rpart.plot)

tree_model <- rpart(popularity ~ danceability + energy + valence + acousticness +
                      instrumentalness + liveness + loudness + speechiness +
                      tempo + duration_min,
                    data = spotify_train_rf,
                    method = "anova")

rpart.plot(tree_model)
```

---

## Random Forest Analysis with Hyperparameter Tuning

A Random Forest model was trained and tuned to improve predictive performance. The best number of randomly selected predictors (`mtry`) was selected using 5-fold cross-validation.

**Why Random Forest?**  
Random Forests aggregate the predictions of many trees, reducing overfitting and capturing complex nonlinear interactions. They tend to perform better than individual trees on unseen data.

**Assumptions:**  
- Data points are independent
- No strict assumptions on distribution or linearity
- Some randomness improves generalization (bagging and random feature selection)

**Limitations:**  
Random Forests sacrifice some interpretability compared to single trees. Additionally, since popularity is influenced by many external factors (e.g., marketing, social media), audio features alone can still only explain part of the variability.


```{r}
library(caret)
library(randomForest)

set.seed(2025)

train_control <- trainControl(method = "cv", number = 5)

rf_caret_model <- train(
  popularity ~ danceability + energy + valence + acousticness +
    instrumentalness + liveness + loudness + speechiness +
    tempo + duration_min,
  data = spotify_train_rf,
  method = "rf",
  trControl = train_control,
  tuneLength = 5
)

rf_caret_model$bestTune

plot(rf_caret_model)
```

---

## Final Random Forest Evaluation on Test Set

The best tuned Random Forest model was evaluated on the held-out test set.


```{r}
predictions_rf <- predict(rf_caret_model, newdata = spotify_test_rf)

test_rmse_rf <- sqrt(mean((spotify_test_rf$popularity - predictions_rf)^2))

test_rmse_rf
```

---

# Final Comparison of Model Performance

The test RMSEs for Linear Regression and Random Forest were compared to assess overall predictive performance.

```{r}
# Create a comparison data frame
rmse_df <- data.frame(
  Model = c("Linear Regression", "Random Forest"),
  RMSE = c(18.22, 17.94)
)

# Plot RMSEs
library(ggplot2)

ggplot(rmse_df, aes(x = Model, y = RMSE)) +
  geom_col(fill = "steelblue") +
  geom_text(aes(label = round(RMSE, 2)), vjust = -0.5) +
  labs(
    title = "Test RMSE Comparison Between Models",
    y = "Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)",
    x = "Model"
  ) +
  theme_minimal()
```

